{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 19, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Fulk and ruled County of Tripoli from 1147 to 1177 he was the son of Walter II Count of Tripoli and Joscelin I Count of Edessa's daughter he succeeded his father as count in 1147\n\n*He played a crucial role in the Second Crusade by warning the crusaders about the perils of the Anatolian route and advising them to take the coastal route instead which would have provided them with easier access to supplies and protection from the Byzantine Empire*\n\nRaymond III was captured by Nur ad-Din Zengi in 1149 during the Battle of Inab and was imprisoned in Aleppo for two years he was released in 1151 after being ransomed by King Baldwin III of Jerusalem he returned to Tripoli and continued to rule the county\n\nHe married Agnes of Courtenay widow of King Amalric I of Jerusalem in 1177 but the marriage was short lived as he died the same year it is believed that he died of natural causes\n\n*The County of Tripoli under Raymond III's rule maintained good relations with the Byzantine Empire and he even received financial support from Emperor Manuel I Comnenus to defend his territory against Muslim forces*\n\nRaymond III was succeeded by William of Tyre as regent for his nephew Hugh III of Cyprus who would later become the King of Jerusalem he is often remembered for his failed attempt to take back the city of Homs from the Muslims\n\n*Raymond III's leadership during the Second Crusade showcased his diplomatic skills as he formed alliances with other Christian leaders to counter the growing Muslim threat in the region however his capture and subsequent release hindered his ability to make a more significant impact on the crusade's outcome*", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.9996897578239441}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.169585604860913e-05}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -8.606540